  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Mid-level shortwave ridge axis is in the \nprocess of crossing overhead across northern MI early this afternoon \nin advance of a weak, moisture-starved trough this evening. This \nquick-moving wave exits stage right tonight with a broader upper \nridge building across the nation's midsection Tuesday though \nWednesday. However, during the day Wednesday, another shortwave \nejects lee of the Rockies and is expected to trek northeast across \nthe northern Plains into southern Manitoba/Ontario by Thursday \nmorning. Attendant occluding sfc low pressure to follow suit, \nperhaps scraping parts of the forecast area with a few showers. \nOtherwise, better chances for rain hold off until Saturday night - \nSunday as another system spins up lee of the Rockies and slides \ntowards the Great Lakes.\n\nForecast Details: Main story for much of the week ahead will be well-\nabove normal temperatures on the heels of occasionally breezy \nsouthwesterly winds. High temps this afternoon in the upper 40s and \nlow 50s, with lots of 50s expected for Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThursday through Saturday even some lower 60s possible in \ndownsloping areas. The best shot at making a run at record breaking \ntemperatures comes on Saturday -- that's if current timing trends \nhold with the weekend system alluded to above...if that system \ntrends any faster, that thought is probably out the window.\n\nIn terms of those rain chances, our next chance arrives on Thursday, \nand it doesn't look promising. While there's expected to be decent \nforcing provided by a shortwave sliding by to our northwest, paltry \nmoisture should really limit areal coverage of any showers. As was \nthe case for the previous shift, can't really justify much more than \nlow PoPs across parts of the eastern U.P. Better chances for rain \narrive this weekend as low pressure develops lee of the Rockies and \ntreks northeast. Still quite a bit of spread on where this system \nactually tracks, whether it's across the upper MS Valley or more \ntoward the Great Lakes. That said, either solution would bring much \nhigher chances for periods of showers, with a cool down behind this \nsystem for late next weekend into the start of next week.",
